WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.270
The only thing better than a good horror movie
is twenty-five of 'em — one after the other,

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:08.870
until you can no longer remember a time when
you weren't screaming and didn't have crippling

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:12.110
jump-scare anxiety.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.680
(screaming)

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:19.660
From creature features to teen scream-a-thons
and indie darlings, these movies are all available

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:22.960
on Netflix — and all guaranteed to scare
your pants off.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.610
Here are the 25 best horror movies to stream
on Netflix right now.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:28.660
The Wailing

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:33.850
The atmospheric, artful thrills of K-horror
meet a tried-and-true tale of demonic possession

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:38.950
in 2016's The Wailing, which follows a police
officer as he tries to uncover the truth about

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:43.570
the mysterious, violent sickness sweeping
through his South Korean mountain village.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.629
Although The Wailing is the slowest of slow
burns, running a ridiculous two hours and

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:53.120
thirty-six minutes, director Hong-jin Na earns
every second of his movie's runtime by ramping

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.960
up the tension to the perfect pitch and pacing
out the scares like a pro.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:58.160
The Babadook

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.100
This entry from Down Under was on everyone's
lists of the best horror films of 2015, and

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.589
it's not hard to see why.

00:01:04.589 --> 00:01:09.340
Tense, atmospheric, visually striking, and
with an Oscar-worthy performance by Noah Wiseman

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.039
as the creepy kid to end all creepy kids,
this story of a single mother battling an

00:01:14.039 --> 00:01:18.450
evil storybook creature called the Babadook
is high-quality nightmare material.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:24.799
As far as ceiling-crawling anti-gravity monsters
go, this guy is chilling.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:27.480
(screaming)

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:28.770
The Unborn

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:34.639
2009 was a dry period for good, original horror
fare, with the exception of The Unborn, which

00:01:34.639 --> 00:01:40.229
has plenty to make it worth a watch: a family-centric,
fetal twist on the usual possession storyline,

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:44.539
top-quality scares and solid production values,
and a surprisingly great cast.

00:01:44.539 --> 00:01:46.659
The Unborn asks all of the important questions.

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:50.820
"Do you think it's possible to be haunted
by someone who was never even born?"

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:56.069
Not only does Oscar-nominated actor Gary Oldman
turn up, but senior citizen scares like this

00:01:56.069 --> 00:01:59.949
one will probably keep you from visiting grandma
for a long, long time.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.600
(creepy moaning)

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:09.399
Sinister

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:14.050
If you've been nostalgic for Ethan Hawke since
his 1990s dreamboat days ended, you'll definitely

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:18.390
dig him in this grown-up role as a skeptical
dad who doesn't quite believe that he's discovered

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.180
a haunted box of Super 8 reels.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:24.400
Until it's too late, of course, and the scares
are coming fast and furious.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:28.790
Released in 2012, Sinister does a nice job
of mashing up the contemporary concept of

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:34.030
found-footage horror with the feel of a classic
haunting flick, and the "home movies" that

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:38.750
are central to the film's plot are genuinely
creepy, from the first scene on.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:39.750
The Host

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:44.629
Before he wowed the world with Snowpiercer,
director Bong Joon-ho was the director of

00:02:44.629 --> 00:02:47.469
the best Korean creature feature of the early
aughts.

00:02:47.469 --> 00:02:52.310
The 2006 film The Host tells the story of
a hapless dad searching for his daughter,

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:56.900
who has been kidnapped by an acrobatic, sewer-dwelling
beast with mayhem on its mind.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:01.109
The movie is as much a Godzilla satire as
it is a straightforward monster flick, and

00:03:01.109 --> 00:03:05.129
the director's trademark slapstick moments
are great tension-relievers in a very, very

00:03:05.129 --> 00:03:06.260
taut thriller.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.299
A Girl Walks Home Alone at Night

00:03:08.299 --> 00:03:13.540
As stylish as she is deadly, the veiled vampire
at the center of Ana Lily Amirpour's debut

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:18.090
feature film is the best kind of bad girl,
and remarkably sympathetic for a person who

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.730
feasts on the blood of the living.

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:30.370
The pacing in this horror flick is a bit slow,
but made up for by the beauty of its leading

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:33.049
lady and its atmospheric black-and-white world.

00:03:33.049 --> 00:03:38.400
When blood spills in Amirpour's setting of
Bad City, the splatter is downright elegant.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.400
Housebound

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:43.790
This Kiwi horror comedy takes a tongue-in-cheek
approach to a classic trope: instead of movie

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:47.739
about a haunted house, it's a movie about
a haunted house arrest.

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:51.780
Small-time criminal Kylie is sentenced to
eight months on her parents' property, which

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.029
happens to have an angry ghost in residence.

00:03:54.029 --> 00:03:58.689
Kylie's ankle bracelet also serves as a clever
answer to the question that plagues most haunted

00:03:58.689 --> 00:04:03.430
horror films — unlike the dopey characters
in other movies who inexplicably stick around

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:07.269
to be terrorized, the heroine of Housebound
can't leave her ghost-ridden residence.

00:04:07.269 --> 00:04:10.639
"Aren't you lucky Kylie, having all that fancy
technology on your foot."

00:04:10.639 --> 00:04:14.939
Choosing between brutal ghost ending and a
prison sentence was never so difficult.

00:04:14.939 --> 00:04:15.939
Bad Milo!

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:18.140
"What is it?"

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.440
"You've got a thing in your butt."

00:04:22.449 --> 00:04:26.610
Thanks to the oversaturated nature of the
horror genre, Bad Milo is not the only movie

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:30.030
you can watch in which a monster
emerges from someone's tushie.

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.220
It is, however, the best of the bunch.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.410
Sorry, Dreamcatcher.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:38.319
Starring longtime comedy icon and The State
veteran Ken Marino, Bad Milo tells the story

00:04:38.319 --> 00:04:43.440
of a man who discovers that his lifelong gastrointestinal
issues are the result of a demonic polyp,

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:46.500
which escapes from his bum to slay his enemies
at will.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.470
Despite its incredibly gross premise, the
movie rises above basic bathroom horror to

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:54.840
be reliably entertaining — and Milo, the
butt gremlin who kinda looks like the baby

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.980
from Dinosaurs, is really pretty lovable.

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:03.679
(adorable butt-monster noises)

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.200
Hush

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:09.750
The ordinary home invasion thriller gets kicked
up a notch by the injection of a unique twist

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.310
in Hush: the heroine is deaf.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:17.630
Low on gore and high on suspense, the 2016
film succeeds despite a limited budget and

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:22.169
almost no dialogue, thanks to its deliberate
storytelling and creative use of sound and

00:05:22.169 --> 00:05:24.030
silence to ramp up the tension.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:28.740
Add in a stellar performance by Kate Siegel,
who also co-wrote the film, and Hush is more

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.970
than worth investing the short 80 minutes
it takes to watch.

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:32.970
Dead Snow

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:35.110
Gory, ghastly, and campy as hell.

00:05:35.110 --> 00:05:39.621
In other words, Dead Snow fulfills all reasonable
expectations for a horror film about zombie

00:05:39.621 --> 00:05:44.360
Nazis rising from a snow-covered hillside
to ruin a perfectly good ski vacation.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:49.180
You won't get nuanced performances or subtle
Hitchcockian tension from this movie; you

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:52.500
will, however, get all the blood-spattered
action you've ever wanted.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:57.289
When will people realize that remote cabins
are absolutely terrible vacation spots?

00:05:57.289 --> 00:05:58.449
Dead Silence

00:05:58.449 --> 00:06:01.649
If you're creeped out by ventriloquist dummies,
and seriously, who isn't?...

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.080
(everyone's reaction to ventriloquist dummies)

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.860
...then you'll thoroughly appreciate this
movie by horror virtuoso James Wan, who delved

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:17.449
into dummy fare in between directing his better-known
projects Saw and Insidious.

00:06:17.449 --> 00:06:22.490
Starring True Blood's Ryan Kwanten, Dead Silence
occasionally veers into cheesy territory,

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:26.490
but it's hard not to when your characters
are battling the ghost of an evil ventriloquist.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:31.060
Fortunately, its premise is unique enough,
and it delivers solid scares.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:32.400
We Are What We Are

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:37.720
In this 2013 film, the Parkers are a close-knit
and reclusive family bound by a mysterious

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:38.860
shared religious tradition.

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:43.680
But rather than draw out that mystery, We
Are What We Are reveals it early on and then

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.530
draws its horrors from the resulting family
drama.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:50.800
There's plenty of blood and guts here, too,
but the American gothic flavor and subtle

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.759
character development elevate it above the
average gorefest.

00:06:54.759 --> 00:06:56.409
Cabin Fever: Patient Zero

00:06:56.409 --> 00:07:00.439
Fans of the original Cabin Fever will like
this film for the same reasons as they loved

00:07:00.439 --> 00:07:01.439
the original.

00:07:01.439 --> 00:07:05.849
It's gruesomely, graphically disgusting, possibly
even more than its predecessors.

00:07:05.849 --> 00:07:10.300
Centering on the origin story of the series'
flesh-eating virus, and the worst bachelor

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:15.919
party in history, Cabin Fever: Patient Zero
reveals the shocking truth.

00:07:15.919 --> 00:07:16.919
Honeymoon

00:07:16.919 --> 00:07:20.720
If you've been missing actress Rose Leslie
since her untimely exit from Game of Thrones,

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:25.669
this slow burn horror flick about a honeymoon
gone wrong will put her back on your TV screen.

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:30.620
Leslie stars opposite Harry Treadaway in a
story about two newlyweds whose post-nuptial

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:35.699
bliss at a secluded cabin is rudely interrupted
by something strange lurking in the woods.

00:07:35.699 --> 00:07:40.830
Without spoiling anything, let's just say
that the honeymoon gets real weird, real fast,

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:42.319
before it's officially over.

00:07:42.319 --> 00:07:43.319
It Follows

00:07:43.319 --> 00:07:47.349
With every classic horror trope having been
done and re-done to hades, it's rare to find

00:07:47.349 --> 00:07:51.139
a movie that looks and feels like something
you've never seen before.

00:07:51.139 --> 00:07:56.129
"Wherever you are, it's somewhere, walking
straight for you.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.560
All you can do is pass it along to someone
else."

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.419
But with its unique premise, slow pacing,
and inventive soundtrack, It Follows is one

00:08:05.419 --> 00:08:09.970
of the creepiest and most original horror
releases in years — a particularly impressive

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:14.009
feat considering its small budget and use
of old-school practical effects to bring the

00:08:14.009 --> 00:08:15.009
scares.

00:08:15.009 --> 00:08:19.400
A million-dollar CGI monster has nothing on
a half-naked, toothless intruder peeing on

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.659
your kitchen floor.

00:08:20.659 --> 00:08:21.780
Take our word for it.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:22.780
The Awakening

00:08:22.780 --> 00:08:27.479
For spooky atmospheric tension and a unique
historic premise, look no further than The

00:08:27.479 --> 00:08:28.550
Awakening.

00:08:28.550 --> 00:08:32.550
Set in the aftermath of the First World War,
this movie follows its heroine to a boarding

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:35.070
school with a ghost problem…or is it?

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:39.330
With its characters as haunted by the horrors
of war as they are by any spirits on campus,

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:43.320
The Awakening is big on suspense, and the
mystery of what kind of evil they're dealing

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.720
with persists almost all the way to the end.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:47.720
V/H/S

00:08:47.720 --> 00:08:51.240
For those with short attention spans, there's
nothing better than a feature-length anthology

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:54.790
of mini-horror-movies—and V/H/S is among
the best.

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:59.820
Using every available found-footage conceit,
from recorded Skype calls to vacation videos,

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:04.840
it tells a series of scary stories that run
the gamut from alien abduction to wilderness

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:06.890
monsters, twenty minutes at a time.

00:09:06.890 --> 00:09:12.580
If you dig the V/H/S anthology, a pair of
sequels in the same format, V/H/S/2 and V/H/S:

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:16.310
Viral, are also available to stream on Netflix.

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:17.310
Troll Hunter

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:22.030
Not to be confused with the original animated
Netflix series Trollhunters, or even the amazing

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:23.370
Troll 2…

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.300
"Oh my goooooodddddddd!"

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:33.000
...Troll Hunter is director André Øvredal's
first horror movie, so catch it while it's

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:34.230
still on Netflix.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:38.690
Set in the remote, forested northern fjords
of Norway, this found-footage mockumentary

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:43.590
follows a group of students as they try to
catch an elusive species of giant troll on

00:09:43.590 --> 00:09:46.320
camera, without being eaten or trampled alive
in the process.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.900
Needless to say, some of the characters are
more successful in that endeavor than others.

00:09:50.900 --> 00:09:51.900
Ravenous

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:57.770
In this 1999 film, a group of mid-19th-century
military frontiersmen save the life of a stranger

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:02.150
who stumbles into their camp… who then thanks
the frontiersmen by having them all for dinner.

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:07.230
"Man eats the flesh of another, he absorbs
the other man's strength…"

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:10.320
(epic, cannibalistic yelling)

00:10:10.600 --> 00:10:14.560
Cannibal hillbilly stories are a dime a dozen,
but Ravenous breathes fresh life into the

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:19.810
trope with an awesome cast, a nifty historical
angle, and some unexpectedly hilarious jokes

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:22.150
about the etiquette of eating your fellow
man.

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:24.050
Tucker and Dale vs. Evil

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:25.880
"Come on!"

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:27.220
"What is going on?"

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:29.440
"How is he even walking right now, Tucker?"

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:30.660
"He looks like he's going to walk it off.

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:31.960
He's going to be fine."

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:35.770
If you're looking to scream with laughter
rather than terror, it doesn't get better

00:10:35.770 --> 00:10:38.890
than this pitch-perfect sendup of the camp
slasher genre.

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:44.040
Tyler Labine and Alan Tudyk star as a pair
of backwoods BFFs who are just trying to enjoy

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:47.100
a relaxing vacation at their fixer-upper cabin
in the mountains.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:52.390
But when a group of preppy, judgmental college
kids sets up camp nearby, a series of absurd

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:58.170
misunderstandings unleash an unstoppable tide
of rage, violence, and accidental endings-by-woodchipper.

00:10:58.170 --> 00:10:59.820
An American Werewolf in London

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:03.760
The best practical special effects you'll
ever see in a creature feature belong to this

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:06.480
classic werewolf movie from 1981.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:11.450
An American Werewolf in London is as darkly
funny as it is terrifying, in a dry, British

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:16.440
sort of way, like when the mangled spirits
of the various victims of David's lunar activities

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:21.380
turn up to scold him for not only slaying
innocent Londoners, but being such an unrefined

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:22.640
boor about it.

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:24.260
"What shall I do?"

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:31.060
They just don't make horror like this anymore…
although they tried, bless their little hearts.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:35.590
Don't confuse this one with the 1997 film,
An American Werewolf in Paris, which was a

00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:37.430
pile of hot garbage.

00:11:37.430 --> 00:11:38.430
Odd Thomas

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:43.480
In this film based on a book by Dean Koontz,
Anton Yelchin plays a small-town fry cook

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:47.860
and part-time vigilante psychic who uses his
gifts to hunt down psychos.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:52.510
He runs into trouble when his town is invaded
by an evil beyond anything he's seen before.

00:11:52.510 --> 00:11:57.330
As horror flicks go, Odd Thomas is more whimsical,
and occasionally sentimental, but Yelchin

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:01.670
is so charming in the title role that you
won't miss the jump scares a bit.

00:12:01.670 --> 00:12:02.670
Hellraiser

00:12:02.670 --> 00:12:06.610
On its surface, Hellraiser is a cautionary
tale about buying and solving puzzle boxes

00:12:06.610 --> 00:12:08.510
from sketchy Moroccan street vendors.

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:13.210
But really, it's all about master of horror
Clive Barker, and his first foray into the

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:16.810
grotesque fantasy world that would spawn close
to a dozen films.

00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:21.231
The acting won't win any awards, and the gore
is over the top even for a horror film, but

00:12:21.231 --> 00:12:26.130
Hellraiser's unique combination of demonic
lore and lingering cringe shots of slimy,

00:12:26.130 --> 00:12:30.730
torn-off people parts makes it a must-watch
for anyone who claims to be serious about

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:31.730
horror.

00:12:31.730 --> 00:12:32.730
The Fly

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:37.830
Everyone loves David Cronenberg's 1986 version
of sci-fi horror story The Fly, but this version

00:12:37.830 --> 00:12:39.850
from 1958 came first.

00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:43.690
It's a classic science fiction film, with
a cast that includes everybody's favorite

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:46.150
cadaverous leading man, Vincent Price.

00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:50.980
As far as the plotline, you know the drill:
mad scientist gets overambitious, has careless

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:56.070
lab accident, transfigures self into gruesome
half-human, half-fly monstrosity.

00:12:56.070 --> 00:13:01.010
But back in the mid-20th century, this movie
was groundbreaking—and even by modern-day

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:04.480
standards, it goes to some shockingly dark
places.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:05.480
The Shining

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:09.160
We know you've seen The Shining, or at least
the thousands of pop culture references to

00:13:09.160 --> 00:13:09.660
it.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:13.450
"No TV and no beer make Homer something something…"

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:15.540
"Go crazy?"

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:17.340
"Don't mind if I do!"

00:13:20.440 --> 00:13:23.900
But no matter how many times you've seen it,
you haven't seen it enough.

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:28.960
Stanley Kubrick's 1980 adaptation of Stephen
King's terrifying haunted-hotel novel was

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:33.070
notoriously loathed by the author, but it's
still a brilliant piece of filmmaking from

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:34.070
a master.

00:13:34.070 --> 00:13:38.080
The Shining packs in plenty of gut-punching
terror, focusing on a horrific performance

00:13:38.080 --> 00:13:41.440
by Jack Nicholson as a man coming undone in
the worst way.

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:45.980
In Kubrick's hands, a slow and lingering visual
crawl down an empty hallway at the Overlook

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:48.650
Hotel is more terrifying than any jump scare.

00:13:48.650 --> 00:13:52.610
But really, see it for the hundreds of pop
culture references that will suddenly make

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:53.660
sense.

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:55.040
"Redrum Redrum!"

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:55.580
"Danny stop it!

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:57.660
Danny?"

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:05.140
"Trab pu kcip!"

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:07.460
"What have we told you about writing on the
